{
  "_comment": "EXAMPLE FILE for the private, versioned RustFS deployment object read by Terrakube. It contains desired state only; credentials and private keys live at native OpenBao KV paths.",
  "proxmox_node": "proxmox-1",
  "environment": "homelab",
  "domain": "example.com",
  "vm_ssh_public_key": "ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIExampleOnly example@example.com",
  "_iso_base_url_comment": "Base URL (no trailing slash) of the read-only object prefix holding OS install media, consumed by modules/proxmox-stack/iso_downloads.tf. Objects are versioned in the key name (Windows11-25H2.iso, virtio-win-0.1.285.iso) and each is sha256-pinned in the resource, so content that does not match is rejected at download time. OPTIONAL: omit it if you do not build templates -- the placeholder default then fails on an unresolvable host rather than fetching something unintended.",
  "iso_base_url": "https://s3.example.com/isos",
  "_llm_large_serving_comment": "Heavy-tier LLM serving host. Both keys are OPTIONAL and exist because this host is a fixed-address reservation made by the network IaC, not a PVE guest -- it never appears in `containers`, so unlike every other host its address cannot be derived as cidrhost(network_cidrs[vlan], vm_id). They are published to the Ansible repositories as constants.serving.host / constants.serving.ip, replacing the copies those repositories used to carry themselves. Omit both if you do not run a heavy tier: consumers report the absence and stop, and that is the intended behaviour -- a substituted default would fail later and somewhere else, as a timeout against an address nobody chose.",
  "llm_large_serving_host": "llm-large-example",
  "llm_large_serving_ip": "192.0.2.10",
  "network_cidrs": {
    "lan_main": "192.0.2.0/24",
    "mgmt": "198.51.100.0/24",
    "mgmt_native": "203.0.113.0/24",
    "dns": "192.0.2.0/24",
    "bmc": "198.51.100.0/24",
    "compute": "203.0.113.0/24",
    "siem": "192.0.2.0/24",
    "pipeline": "198.51.100.0/24",
    "data": "203.0.113.0/24",
    "ai": "192.0.2.0/24",
    "apps": "198.51.100.0/24",
    "media_svc": "203.0.113.0/24",
    "homeauto": "192.0.2.0/24",
    "nonprod": "198.51.100.0/24"
  },
  "_nodes_comment": "Proxmox cluster nodes (non-secret identity). proxmox_node above is the primary/default placement. commissioned=false means declared but not yet installed. services_enabled=false gates per-node (\"DaemonSet-style\") service placement only -- an already-commissioned node mid a storage rebuild keeps commissioned=true but sets services_enabled=false until the rebuild finishes; everything else it already runs is unaffected.",
  "nodes": {
    "proxmox-1": {
      "role": "node-1",
      "hardware": "amd-desktop"
    },
    "proxmox-2": {
      "role": "node-2",
      "hardware": "dell-r410",
      "services_enabled": false
    },
    "proxmox-3": {
      "role": "node-3",
      "hardware": "dell-r710"
    },
    "proxmox-4": {
      "role": "node-4",
      "hardware": "dell-r710"
    }
  },
  "proxmox_iso_debian": "debian-13.2.0-amd64-netinst.iso",
  "proxmox_ct_template_debian": "debian-13-standard_13.1-2_amd64.tar.zst",
  "ansible_cloud_init_file": "cloud-init/ansible-server-example.yml",
  "splunk_vm_id": 110,
  "splunk_vm_name": "splunk-aio",
  "splunk_vm_pool_id": "logging",
  "template_id": 9100,
  "splunk_boot_disk_size": 25,
  "splunk_data_disk_size": 200,
  "_splunk_placement_comment": "Placement for the all-in-one Splunk VM. splunk_node_name was previously hardwired to proxmox_node, so the indexer could not be placed anywhere but the primary node. Relocating it has THREE prerequisites and they are ordered: (1) splunk_cpu_type must leave \"host\" \u2014 a host-CPU guest cannot move between CPU vendors, because the feature set it booted with does not exist on the destination; x86-64-v2 is portable to every node here, x86-64-v3 where every candidate node has AVX2. The change needs a guest reboot to take effect, so verify the VM is healthy on the portable model BEFORE going further. (2) splunk_migrate must be true \u2014 otherwise a splunk_node_name change is a destroy-and-recreate, which destroys the index volumes. (3) Every storage id the VM's disks target must exist on the destination node; a node-scoped pool id is the real constraint on where this VM can go, independent of CPU and memory.",
  "splunk_node_name": "proxmox-1",
  "splunk_cpu_type": "host",
  "splunk_migrate": false,
  "pools": {
    "logging": {
      "comment": "Logging and observability infrastructure"
    },
    "infrastructure": {
      "comment": "Core infrastructure services"
    },
    "ai": {
      "comment": "AI orchestration tier (n8n, Dify, LangFlow, LangGraph, agent-exec)"
    },
    "security": {
      "comment": "Honeypots and deception sensors (OpenCanary tripwires, T-Pot, apprise notify gateway)"
    }
  },
  "datastores": {},
  "_node_storage_comment": "Per-node ZFS storage DECLARATION. Terraform does NOT create ZFS pools (zpool create is OS-level). ansible-proxmox reads this via the ansible_inventory output to create pools/datasets/quotas and (when register=true) `pvesm add zfspool`. Adapt pool names and RAID levels for your hardware.",
  "node_storage": {
    "proxmox-2": {
      "pools": {
        "example-pool": {
          "type": "zfspool",
          "raid": "raidz1",
          "protected": true,
          "register": true,
          "content": [
            "images",
            "rootdir"
          ],
          "datasets": {
            "backups": {
              "quota": "1T",
              "properties": {
                "compression": "zstd"
              }
            },
            "databases": {
              "quota": "1T",
              "nfs_export": "ro=@10.0.0.0/8",
              "properties": {
                "recordsize": "16K",
                "atime": "off",
                "compression": "zstd"
              }
            },
            "data": {
              "quota": "5T",
              "properties": {
                "recordsize": "1M",
                "compression": "zstd",
                "com.sun:auto-snapshot": "false"
              }
            },
            "appdata": {
              "quota": "100G",
              "properties": {
                "recordsize": "16K",
                "atime": "off",
                "compression": "zstd"
              }
            },
            "appdata/plex": {},
            "appdata/sonarr": {},
            "appdata/radarr": {},
            "appdata/prowlarr": {},
            "appdata/seerr": {},
            "appdata/sortarr": {}
          }
        },
        "example-nvme": {
          "type": "zfspool",
          "raid": "mirror",
          "protected": true,
          "register": false,
          "content": [
            "images",
            "rootdir"
          ],
          "_splunk_fast_comment": "fast-splunk hot/warm tier. The `pvesm_id` registers this child dataset as its own first-class Proxmox storage id `fast-splunk`, which the Splunk VM's virtio2 disk targets directly (a plain quota would land the disk at the pool root instead). In the real topology the fast tier lives on the fast/NVMe node.",
          "datasets": {
            "splunk": {
              "quota": "1T",
              "pvesm_id": "fast-splunk",
              "properties": {
                "recordsize": "128K",
                "compression": "lz4"
              }
            },
            "siem/splunk-hot": {
              "quota": "300G",
              "properties": {
                "recordsize": "128K",
                "compression": "lz4"
              }
            },
            "siem/cribl-pq": {
              "quota": "50G",
              "properties": {
                "compression": "lz4",
                "com.sun:auto-snapshot": "false"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        "_bulk_splunk_comment": "bulk-splunk cold tier: a DEDICATED non-RAID pool (raid: \"\"), separate from the raidz1 example-pool because ZFS RAID is pool-wide \u2014 a non-RAID cold tier cannot share a raidz pool. protected:false + no snapshots + backup=false on its VM disk (see tofu) is intentional: durability comes from the Backblaze B2 frozen archive, never vzdump. HUMAN ACTION: the physical spare disk (/dev/disk/by-id/...) for this pool must be confirmed/supplied before ansible-proxmox can create it. register:true + the `data` dataset's pvesm_id publish the `bulk-splunk` storage id the Splunk VM's virtio3 disk targets.",
        "bulk-splunk": {
          "type": "zfspool",
          "raid": "",
          "protected": false,
          "register": true,
          "content": [
            "images"
          ],
          "datasets": {
            "data": {
              "quota": "2T",
              "pvesm_id": "bulk-splunk",
              "properties": {
                "com.sun:auto-snapshot": "false"
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "proxmox-3": {
      "pools": {
        "example-bulk-pool": {
          "type": "zfspool",
          "raid": "raidz2",
          "register": false,
          "content": [
            "images",
            "rootdir"
          ],
          "datasets": {
            "backups": {
              "quota": "1T"
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  },
  "host_services": {},
  "_openbao_cluster_comment": "OpenBao Raft HA peers generated by OpenTofu before Terraform receives inputs. Suffixes drive all peer identity: openbao-10 -> vm_id 110010 and mgmt host .10, openbao-20 -> vm_id 110020 and mgmt host .20, etc. Keep shared LXC config in container_defaults; placement should carry only node -> suffix identity. SPREAD, not count, is what survives a node loss: place voters so that losing any single node still leaves at least one voter above quorum (floor(N/2)+1) \u2014 the openbao_voter_concentration check enforces this, and note that adding ONE voter to a lopsided cluster buys nothing because quorum rises with it. Suffix 40 is reserved: it collides with the vm_id of an explicitly declared openbao container, so a fourth node starts at 41. The generator does not filter on commissioned: a node must be commissioned and carry a registered ZFS pool BEFORE its suffixes are added here, or the apply creates guests on a node that cannot host them.",
  "openbao_cluster": {
    "enabled": true,
    "name_prefix": "openbao-",
    "vm_id_base": 110000,
    "vlan": "mgmt",
    "placement": {
      "proxmox-1": [
        10
      ],
      "proxmox-2": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "proxmox-3": [
        30,
        31
      ],
      "proxmox-4": [
        41,
        42
      ]
    },
    "container_defaults": {
      "unprivileged": true,
      "description": "OpenBao secrets manager - Raft voter",
      "cpu_cores": 2,
      "memory_dedicated": 2048,
      "memory_swap": 0,
      "tags": [
        "terraform",
        "container",
        "openbao",
        "secrets",
        "infrastructure"
      ],
      "pool_id": "infrastructure"
    },
    "root_disk": {
      "size": 10
    },
    "root_disk_datastore_by_node": {
      "proxmox-2": "example-pool",
      "proxmox-3": "example-bulk-pool"
    }
  },
  "_node_services_comment": "Per-node (\"DaemonSet-style\") service templates -- one container per node listed in per_node, generated by main.tf's node_service_containers instead of a hand-copied block per node. A node is skipped if it is not commissioned, has services_enabled=false, or has no per_node entry here (vm_id/host_octet are reserved-octet allocations, never derived by formula -- an unlisted node has no safe address to assign). Traefik is the first consumer; a future per-node service (e.g. a technitium secondary) is a new top-level key here, no code change.",
  "node_services": {
    "agentgateway": {
      "name_prefix": "agentgateway-",
      "vlan": "ai",
      "dhcp": true,
      "per_node": {
        "proxmox-1": {
          "suffix": 10,
          "vm_id": 516000
        },
        "proxmox-3": {
          "suffix": 30,
          "vm_id": 516010
        },
        "proxmox-4": {
          "suffix": 40,
          "vm_id": 516020
        }
      },
      "_per_node_comment": "MCP-fabric HA: one stateless instance per always-on node, pooled behind the mcp.<domain> / agentgateway.<domain> Traefik routes (agentgateway_backends). Full DHCP (DNS-first FQDN addressing) \u2014 static assignment, if ever wanted, is UniFi's job driven by the Nautobot SSOT, never a tofu-side octet. proxmox-2 intentionally absent (storage rebuild owns it \u2014 no reserved vm_id until handed back). proxmox-4's entry only expands once that node is commissioned && services_enabled. A down node is dropped by the route health check; a known-dead node is gated with services_enabled=false so applies stay green.",
      "container_defaults": {
        "description": "agentgateway MCP/LLM/A2A data-plane proxy \u2014 unified AI traffic layer routing MCP, OpenAI-compat LLM calls, and A2A messages to internal fabric + external providers (Docker-in-LXC, per-node MCP-fabric HA instance)",
        "cpu_cores": 2,
        "memory_dedicated": 1024,
        "tags": [
          "terraform",
          "container",
          "ai",
          "agentgateway",
          "docker"
        ],
        "pool_id": "ai",
        "unprivileged": true,
        "root_disk": {
          "size": 8
        },
        "features": {
          "nesting": true
        }
      }
    },
    "apt-cacher-ng": {
      "name_prefix": "apt-cacher-ng-",
      "vlan": "apps",
      "dhcp": true,
      "per_node": {
        "proxmox-1": {
          "suffix": 10,
          "vm_id": 613010
        },
        "proxmox-3": {
          "suffix": 30,
          "vm_id": 613030
        }
      },
      "_per_node_comment": "APT proxy HA: one cache per node that hosts package-installing guests, so a node restart cannot take apt down estate-wide. Each instance is an independent cache \u2014 there is no shared state to replicate, which is what makes this service a natural fit for the per-node pattern rather than a clustered one. Clients reach the pool by name and rely on apt trying the next address for the host on a connect failure, so no client carries an instance address. VMIDs decode as apps(6) - mgmt(1) - crit3 - LXC(0) - instance == node id - prd(0), matching how the openbao voters put the node id in the instance digit.",
      "container_defaults": {
        "description": "apt-cacher-ng \u2014 APT package caching proxy (per-node instance)",
        "cpu_cores": 1,
        "memory_dedicated": 512,
        "tags": [
          "terraform",
          "container",
          "infrastructure",
          "apt-cache"
        ],
        "pool_id": "infrastructure",
        "root_disk": {
          "size": 16
        }
      }
    },
    "traefik": {
      "name_prefix": "traefik-",
      "vlan": "mgmt",
      "per_node": {
        "proxmox-1": {
          "suffix": 10,
          "vm_id": 101,
          "host_octet": 101
        },
        "proxmox-3": {
          "suffix": 30,
          "vm_id": 107,
          "host_octet": 107
        }
      },
      "_per_node_comment": "proxmox-2 is intentionally absent here, not just gated by services_enabled=false: it is mid a storage rebuild (a separate, owning migration effort) and gets no reserved vm_id/host_octet -- not even a placeholder -- until that effort hands it back. Add its entry then, alongside flipping services_enabled back to true.",
      "container_defaults": {
        "description": "Traefik reverse proxy / TLS ingress (per-node ingress HA instance)",
        "cpu_cores": 1,
        "memory_dedicated": 512,
        "tags": [
          "terraform",
          "container",
          "ingress",
          "traefik"
        ],
        "pool_id": "infrastructure",
        "root_disk": {
          "size": 8
        }
      }
    }
  },
